# SECTION A — EXISTING APPLICATION AND IMPLEMENTATION RULES

## A1. Inspect the Existing Project First

Before editing files, inspect the actual codebase and identify:

- The existing frontend framework and application entry points.
- Customer booking pages, booking confirmation pages, ride history, active ride screens, and notification components.
- Driver dashboard, ride requests, active trip console, earnings, and availability controls.
- Admin dashboard, driver approval workflows, ride management, and payment verification features.
- Existing Express routes, controllers, services, repositories, authentication middleware, and error handling.
- PostgreSQL/Supabase database schema, migration runner, and existing ride/payment tables.
- Existing Stripe integration, webhook handlers, payment records, and refund handling.
- Existing Clerk authentication and role-resolution logic.
- Google Maps address validation, route calculation, and service-area restrictions.
- Existing realtime subscriptions, polling, and notification delivery.
- Existing ride cancellation, incentive, OTP, and driver commission calculations.

Reuse existing implementations wherever appropriate. Do not create duplicate payment services, competing ride tables, duplicate notification systems, or a second independent booking lifecycle.

If a required component already exists, extend it instead of replacing it unnecessarily.

## A2. Preserve Existing RideNova Functionality

Keep existing live booking, ride cancellation, five-minute live-request timeout, real-time driver acceptance updates, customer driver details, trip OTP verification, service-area validation, and existing authentication.

The five-minute timeout must remain applicable to immediate/on-demand ride requests according to their current rules. Do not apply that timeout to scheduled advance bookings.

Keep the existing white, red, and black visual identity:

- White: #FFFFFF
- Primary red: #E21D2B
- Dark red: #BE1622
- Main text: #171717
- Secondary text: #737373
- Borders: #E5E5E5
- Soft red backgrounds: #FFF1F2

Maintain a premium visual hierarchy, clear status badges, readable typography, consistent icons, useful loading states, accessible controls, and responsive layouts.

Do not introduce fake bookings, fake drivers, fake payment confirmations, fake earnings, or hardcoded success responses.

## A3. Mandatory Implementation Standard

Make changes in both frontend and backend wherever required.

Every new button must perform a real action. Every displayed booking status must come from persisted server data. Every payment status must come from verified payment processing. Every driver assignment must refer to a real authenticated driver.

Do not report success just because a component compiles or an API endpoint exists. Verify the complete user journey.

If a required external service or environment variable is missing, implement the appropriate integration and report the missing configuration honestly. Never simulate a successful payment to make a test or screen appear complete.

