# SECTION E — CUSTOMER PAYMENT PROOF AND REFERENCE

## E1. Keep Payment Proof Distinct from Payment Confirmation

If the existing business workflow requires a customer to upload a payment receipt or provide a transaction reference, implement this as supporting evidence only.

When the selected online payment provider already verifies the payment directly, the provider’s verified transaction remains authoritative. A screenshot must never replace provider verification.

If an administrator needs a reference for reconciliation, display the provider’s transaction or payment reference from the persisted payment record. Do not ask the customer to invent or re-enter a reference that the provider has already supplied.

If the existing payment method legitimately requires manual review, allow a reference and proof upload through a clearly labelled, secure workflow. Do not represent this as automatic provider confirmation.

## E2. Secure Proof Uploads

If a proof image is required by the configured workflow:

- Accept only supported image formats.
- Validate file type and file size on the backend.
- Store files in a private storage location.
- Use access-controlled signed URLs or equivalent protected access.
- Associate the file with the booking, payment attempt, and authenticated customer.
- Prevent unauthorised users from reading or replacing the file.
- Record the upload time and uploader.
- Allow an authorised administrator to review the evidence.
- Avoid logging sensitive payment information.

Provide upload progress, preview, retry, remove-before-submission, and clear error states.

If a verified Stripe payment is used and no additional manual proof is necessary, do not force the customer to upload a redundant screenshot just to complete checkout.

