# SECTION H — SCHEDULING ENGINE AND AUTOMATIC DISPATCH

## H1. Reliable Background Processing

Implement a reliable server-side scheduling mechanism for future pickups.

Do not rely exclusively on an open browser tab, a frontend timer, or a driver keeping the dashboard open.

Use the project’s existing job runner if available. Otherwise, implement a reliable database-backed scheduler or background worker appropriate for the deployment environment.

The scheduler must:

- Find approved scheduled bookings approaching their pickup time.
- Confirm that the booking is still valid.
- Confirm that it has not been cancelled, refunded, or completed.
- Check that an assigned driver is still eligible.
- Trigger assignment or dispatch according to the configured policy.
- Notify the customer and driver when action is required.
- Record job execution and errors.
- Retry recoverable failures safely.
- Avoid duplicate notifications or duplicate dispatch operations.
- Recover overdue jobs after a server restart or deployment.

Make the scheduling logic idempotent. Running a job twice must not create duplicate assignments or alter a completed booking.

## H2. Configurable Dispatch Window

Implement a configurable dispatch window before pickup. Use a proposed initial default of 60 minutes before the scheduled pickup time, adjustable by authorised administrators.

If the customer books within the minimum advance-notice period, apply the same eligibility rules without creating an impossible or expired dispatch window.

Use the dispatch window to begin targeted driver assignment and notify eligible drivers. The booking may be assigned earlier if the platform’s policy permits and an eligible driver accepts.

Do not promise guaranteed driver arrival simply because the customer has paid or the admin has approved the booking.

Use honest UI language such as “Approved — finding a driver” or “Driver assigned.” If no driver is available, show the real status and provide a support or cancellation path.

## H3. No Five-Minute Live-Ride Timeout

Scheduled bookings must not automatically cancel five minutes after the request is submitted.

Keep the existing five-minute timeout for live/on-demand requests only.

Scheduled bookings use their scheduled pickup time, payment-review deadline, cancellation rules, and dispatch policy.

If payment is never completed, expire the unpaid booking according to a configurable payment-session expiry policy. Do not confuse payment expiry with pickup time or the live ride timeout.

If admin review is delayed beyond a configurable deadline, alert the admin and show the customer that review is pending. Do not falsely label the booking as confirmed.

